---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

import SurfaceVoxelGridBasic from "../../components/SurfaceVoxelGridBasic.astro";
import PlaneCubeIntersectionBasic from "../../components/PlaneCubeIntersectionBasic.astro";
import ColumnSurfaceConstruction from "../../components/ColumnSurfaceConstruction.astro";

export const article = {
	title: "XZ Columnar Terrain",
	description:
		"An experiment in constructing a sealed, voxel-owned terrain surface from heightmap-derived planar patches and vertical side fragments.",
	area: "Terrain Systems",
	areaHref: "/areas/terrain-systems",
	published: "2026-10-19",
	draft: false,
};
---

<BaseLayout title={`${article.title} | Aaron's Dev Experiments`}>
	<article class="article-page">
		<header class="article-header">
			<p class="eyebrow">
				<a href={article.areaHref}>{article.area}</a>
			</p>

			<h1>{article.title}</h1>

			<p class="intro">
				{article.description}
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="article-content">
			<p>
				This experiment began with a very simple idea. I wanted to bring
				the visual world of games like <i>Terraria</i>, <i>Starbound</i
				>, and <i>RimWorld</i> into full 3D.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--image">
				<img
					src="/images/rimworld.jpg"
					alt="An overhead view of a RimWorld colony with terrain, buildings, crops, and characters arranged on a tile-based world."
				/>

				<figcaption>
					<em>RimWorld</em> presents its world as a collection of discrete,
					grid-aligned units while still allowing the environment to read
					as a continuous landscape. Screenshot source:
					{" "}
					<a
						href="https://www.gamestar.de/galerien/rimworld,99361.html"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						GameStar
					</a>.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				One of the things that makes games like those unique is that
				they make their respective worlds readable as discrete units.
				You can look at the environment and immediately understand that
				it is made from units of “stuff” that can be mined, traversed,
				placed, or otherwise interacted with individually.
			</p>

			<p>
				Minecraft is the obvious 3D version of that idea. Its blocks
				make the structure of the world completely explicit. But I
				wanted to see whether I could reach beyond a block-based world
				while preserving that same sense of discrete, editable terrain.
			</p>

			<p>
				That led to an interesting question:{" "}
				<i>
					how do you create a three-dimensional world that feels tiled
					and discrete without making everything look like a cube?
				</i>
			</p>

			<p>
				My preliminary answer was to keep voxels as the editable spatial
				structure, and sculpt the surface. I was comfortable keeping
				voxels underneath, but I did not want the player to see a world
				made from exposed voxel faces. Instead, I wanted the terrain
				itself to resolve into deliberate, tile-like pieces.
			</p>

			<h2>The Setup</h2>

			<p>
				To achieve that goal, I chose to start with a continuous terrain
				surface in 3D—a heightmap at first, though I eventually wanted
				to generalize the approach to density fields so it could support
				things like caves and overhangs.
			</p>

			<p>
				In the experiment, the noise started at a scale of one sample
				per 20 world units and used five octaves. Each successive octave
				doubled the frequency while contributing half as much amplitude
				as the previous one. The combined result was normalized and
				scaled to produce the final terrain height.
			</p>

			<p>
				The important property for the rest of the experiment was that
				this produced a continuous height function. For any XZ position,
				I could evaluate a corresponding terrain height. I could also
				sample nearby positions to estimate the local slope of the
				surface.
			</p>

			<p>
				On top of that continuous surface, I would impose a readable
				tiled structure. Each visible piece would still feel like an
				individual unit of terrain, while the landscape as a whole would
				be allowed to resemble a natural environment.
			</p>

			<p>
				That is where the experiment quickly became a geometry problem.
				Taken together, the setup was a continuous terrain surface
				flowing through a discrete voxel lattice, as shown in the
				visualization below. The visible surface therefore had to
				reconcile those two representations.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--visualization">
				<SurfaceVoxelGridBasic />

				<figcaption>
					The highlighted voxels are those intersected by the
					continuous terrain surface. Some contain broad surface
					patches, while others contain only small fragments where the
					surface clips a corner or edge.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				However, in this situation, what should count as one tile—how do
				you construct one? The obvious choice is to use the individual
				voxels that contain the surface as a basis. As I discovered,
				though, that choice introduces several issues.
			</p>

			<h2>The Tile Problem</h2>

			<p>
				There are several competing constraints in an approach like this
				(though some were admittedly self-imposed for what I was trying
				to achieve):
			</p>

			<ul>
				<li>
					As stated previously, you need some geometric basis for
					constructing each tile—how do you use the voxel lattice and
					the surface to create them?
				</li>

				<li>
					Whatever you do with the visible surface, it still has to
					map back to voxels in the end. That voxel ownership is what
					makes the terrain editable by the player.
				</li>

				<li>
					The resulting tiles still have to fit together into a
					reasonably coherent terrain surface.
				</li>

				<li>
					Because terrain would need to be loaded, unloaded, and
					rebuilt in real time, tile construction should be explicitly
					local. Each tile should be constructible using only its own
					voxel and the portion of the continuous surface passing
					through it, without polling neighboring geometry.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<p>
				My first real approach was to approximate the terrain inside
				each surface voxel with a simple tangent plane. I found the
				points where the continuous surface intersected the voxel
				boundary and used their centroid as a representative point for
				the local surface. Combined with the local surface normal, that
				was enough to define a plane that locally approximated the
				terrain inside the voxel.
			</p>

			<p>
				But defining the plane was only part of the problem. The visible
				tile itself could not extend infinitely—it had to be constrained
				by the voxel boundary. So the actual tile came from intersecting
				that local plane with the voxel cube.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--visualization">
				<PlaneCubeIntersectionBasic />

				<figcaption>
					A plane intersecting a cube can produce many different
					bounded polygons depending on its orientation and offset.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				As you can see in the visualization above, this is where the
				approach started to break down. My goal was to create
				rectangular tiles, but a plane/cube intersection does not
				reliably give you that. Depending on how the plane passes
				through the voxel, the resulting tile might be a triangle,
				quadrilateral, pentagon, or hexagon. Worse still, it can also
				shrink down into a tiny fragment near an edge or corner.
			</p>

			<p>
				So even though the geometry was valid, it did not give me the
				kind of consistent tile structure I was looking for. If each
				voxel was allowed to define its own tile this way, the terrain
				surface would be made from pieces with wildly different shapes
				and sizes.
			</p>

			<p>
				The problem was not purely aesthetic either. Those irregular
				pieces also prevented this method of construction from producing
				a sealed terrain surface. Neighboring pieces did not meet in a
				way that completely covered the terrain, resulting in holes.
			</p>

			<p>
				That led me to start thinking about the problem in terms of
				{" "}<em>surface coverage</em>. I knew that planar
				approximations were never going to reproduce the continuous
				surface perfectly, but I didn't necessarily need them to. What I
				needed was a construction that stayed close enough to the
				original surface while reliably covering the terrain's XZ
				footprint. Small discrepancies between neighboring pieces could
				potentially be sealed afterward.
			</p>

			<p>
				This was an important shift in the way I approached the problem.
				Instead of treating the terrain as a completely general 3D
				surface, I was now deliberately giving the vertical axis a
				special role and prioritizing horizontal coverage.
			</p>

			<h2>From Voxels to Columns</h2>

			<p>
				The key change was to stop asking an individual surface voxel to
				define the footprint of a tile. Instead, I began treating each
				XZ voxel position as a vertical column through the terrain.
			</p>

			<p>
				That gave me something the previous approach could not: a fixed,
				rectangular tile domain. Rather than letting whatever fragment
				of the surface happened to fall inside a voxel determine the
				tile boundary, I could define the footprint first and then
				consider the continuous terrain across that entire region.
			</p>

			<p>
				That also gave me more surface information to work with when
				choosing the tile's representative plane. The next question was
				how to reduce that richer information back down to a single
				planar tile.
			</p>

			<h2>Constructing the Surface</h2>

			<p>
				With the tile footprint fixed, I also changed how I chose the
				representative plane. Rather than deriving its tangent point
				from the intersection between the terrain and an individual
				voxel, I could evaluate the heightmap directly at the center of
				the XZ footprint and construct a tangent plane from the height
				and local slope there.
			</p>

			<p>
				That gave me a simple and consistent way to construct each tile,
				but it still had an obvious limitation: the entire plane was
				being decided by what the terrain happened to be doing at a
				single point. Everything else happening across the footprint was
				effectively ignored.
			</p>

			<p>
				I also briefly experimented with using the terrain heights at
				the four corners of the footprint directly. That gave a better
				picture of how the surface behaved across the tile, but
				introduced another problem: four points sampled from a curved
				surface are not generally coplanar. I could fit a plane to them,
				but once again I would be throwing away some of the information
				I had just gained.
			</p>

			<p>
				What I really needed was some kind of intermediate
				representation that could use all of that local information
				before I reduced it back down to a plane. I also had the sense
				that the way the surface was sloped across the footprint needed
				to factor into the result—not just height information. My
				initial line of reasoning was to somehow combine or average the
				gradients around the edges of the XZ footprint.
			</p>

			<p>
				That line of thinking eventually led me to a bicubic Hermite
				patch, which is something I had admittedly never seen before
				this experiment. It gave me a way to preserve both height and
				slope information across the footprint instead of reducing that
				information too early.
			</p>

			<p>
				For each corner of the footprint, I sampled the terrain height
				and estimated its slope in X and Z. Those values defined the
				Hermite patch across the cell. I then evaluated the patch at its
				center and used the resulting height and gradient to define the
				final tangent plane.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--visualization">
				<ColumnSurfaceConstruction />

				<figcaption>
					The direct tangent uses the height and slope of the original
					heightmap at the center of the cell. The Hermite tangent
					uses the height and slope evaluated from a bicubic patch
					built from information across the entire footprint.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				The patch itself was never meant to become the visible terrain.
				It was simply a way to gather more of the local surface behavior
				before reducing everything back down to a planar tile.
			</p>

			<p>
				In practice, that also helped the resulting planes follow the
				underlying continuous surface more closely. Because the Hermite
				patch incorporated information from across the footprint, its
				center height and slope tended to produce a plane that better
				reflected the broader shape of the terrain than one based only
				on a direct sample at the center. And, depending on the terrain,
				the two planes could differ noticeably.
			</p>

			<h2>Voxel Ownership and Sealing</h2>

			<p>
				At this point I had a consistent way to construct one planar
				surface across an XZ footprint, but the plane did not
				necessarily belong to a single voxel in Y.
			</p>

			<p>
				If the underlying surface was steep enough, the planar tile
				could extend up through the voxel column over many voxels. And
				because the terrain was still meant to be editable at the voxel
				level, I needed to preserve that ownership rather than treating
				the entire plane as one indivisible piece.
			</p>

			<p>
				Chunk boundaries made that ownership question more important. A
				single planar tile could cross not only several voxels in Y, but
				also a Y-chunk boundary. Its pieces therefore had to be assigned
				to the voxel and chunk that actually contained them.
			</p>

			<p>
				The solution was to slice the plane wherever it crossed a
				horizontal voxel boundary. Each resulting surface fragment could
				then be assigned to the voxel whose vertical range contained it.
				The same rule naturally carried across chunk boundaries, keeping
				the ownership model local and predictable.
			</p>

			<p>
				With ownership handled, the remaining issue was continuity
				between neighboring columns. The columnar construction gave me
				reliable horizontal coverage across XZ, but adjacent tiles were
				still built independently. Their shared edges would often end at
				different heights, leaving vertical gaps between them.
			</p>

			<p>
				This brought the surface-coverage problem back in a different
				form. I handled those gaps by generating side fragments along
				the boundaries between neighboring columns.
			</p>

			<p>
				Those fragments acted like vertical skirts, connecting the edge
				of one planar tile to the edge of the next. Together with the
				sliced top pieces, they turned the independently constructed
				column surfaces into a sealed terrain surface.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--image">
				<div class="article-image-stack">
					<img
						src="/images/xz-columnar-normals.jpg"
						alt="XZ columnar terrain rendered with normal-based coloring, making the vertical sealing fragments easy to distinguish from the sloped top surfaces."
					/>

					<img
						src="/images/xz-columnar-lit.jpg"
						alt="The same XZ columnar terrain view rendered with lighting and terrain textures."
					/>
				</div>

				<figcaption>
					The same terrain shown with normal-based coloring and with
					lit terrain textures. In the normal view, the vertically
					oriented side fragments stand out clearly between the sloped
					top pieces, making the sealing structure easier to see.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				Sealing did weaken one of my original locality goals. A column
				could no longer be processed in complete isolation, because
				determining what needed to be filled along an edge required
				comparing it against the neighboring column.
			</p>

			<p>
				I still wanted to avoid making chunk construction depend
				directly on neighboring chunks, so each chunk-column grid
				included a one-cell halo around its owned XZ region. Those halo
				cells were constructed from the same underlying heightmap and
				provided the neighboring surface information needed for edge
				comparisons, including along chunk boundaries.
			</p>

			<h2>Where It Broke Down</h2>

			<p>
				By this point, the experiment was doing most of what I had set
				out to do. The terrain was built from discrete, voxel-owned
				pieces, neighboring columns could be sealed together, and the
				result still followed the underlying continuous surface
				reasonably well.
			</p>

			<p>
				But getting there had also changed the nature of the system.
				What started as a more general voxel-surface idea had become
				something much more specifically columnar, and that introduced
				several tradeoffs.
			</p>

			<p>
				The first was axis asymmetry. X and Z defined the tile
				footprint, while Y became the direction in which each tile was
				sliced, assigned, and sealed. That worked naturally for
				heightmap terrain, but it also meant the three axes were no
				longer being treated equally. The approach had become much less
				suitable for arbitrary 3D surfaces. That also worked against my
				original hope of eventually extending the approach from
				heightmaps to more general density-field terrain.
			</p>

			<p>
				The fixed XZ footprint also became increasingly awkward on steep
				terrain. Every tile covered the same horizontal area regardless
				of slope, so a tile spanning a steep section of terrain had to
				stretch farther through world space than one on flatter ground.
				The result could be long surface pieces that no longer read as
				especially tile-like.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--image">
				<img
					src="/images/xz-columnar-steep-slope-distortion.jpg"
					alt="A steep mountainside in the XZ columnar terrain experiment, where the planar terrain tiles appear stretched into long pieces as they climb the slope."
				/>

				<figcaption>
					On steep terrain, the fixed XZ footprint causes the planar
					top pieces to stretch through world space. The tiles remain
					consistent in horizontal coverage, but they become elongated
					and less tile-like as the slope increases.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				The most visible problem showed up where the underlying terrain
				changed slope quickly. Because each tile was still a flat planar
				approximation, neighboring tiles could end up with noticeably
				different orientations. Instead of following the continuous
				surface smoothly through those regions, the mesh developed sharp
				ridges where one planar approximation gave way to the next.
			</p>

			<p>
				That effect was often exaggerated because adjacent top pieces
				did not necessarily meet cleanly at their shared boundary. One
				tile could project farther than the other, creating a staggered
				edge on top of the slope change itself. The surface was sealed,
				but it could still look jagged in exactly the places where the
				original terrain was changing shape most quickly.
			</p>

			<figure class="article-figure article-figure--image">
				<img
					src="/images/xz-columnar-jagged-slope-change.jpg"
					alt="A close-up of the XZ columnar terrain experiment showing sharp, jagged transitions between neighboring planar tiles on a snowy mountainside."
				/>

				<figcaption>
					Where the underlying terrain changes slope quickly,
					neighboring planar tiles can diverge noticeably. Instead of
					forming a smooth transition, the surface develops sharp
					ridges and staggered edges between adjacent pieces.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				That also made the mesh a poor candidate for traversal or
				collision. In the same places where the visible surface became
				jagged, the geometry could create abrupt ridges and awkward
				transitions for a character to move across. A smoother collision
				surface could be generated separately, but then the rendered
				terrain and the surface the player actually moved over would no
				longer agree very well. Characters would appear to clip through
				or otherwise ignore parts of the visible geometry.
			</p>

			<p>
				There was also a locality tradeoff. Sealing required neighboring
				surface information, so the construction was no longer
				completely local at the individual-column level, even though the
				halo kept chunk construction independent.
			</p>

			<p>
				Taken together, those tradeoffs gave me a much clearer sense of
				where this approach worked well and where it started to strain.
				More importantly, they gave me some ideas I can carry forward.
			</p>

			<h2>What I Took From It</h2>

			<p>
				In the end, this was more of an exploration. I started off with
				a somewhat vague idea, followed it to one of its logical
				conclusions, and ended up with what I have described above.
			</p>

			<p>
				I don't think I will be using this exact terrain construction
				for anything I currently want to build, but I can still imagine
				cases where it could make sense. Something closer to a digital
				board game, for example, could probably make better use of the
				strongly tiled surface—especially if character movement was
				designed around the geometry rather than expected to behave like
				ordinary free movement across terrain.
			</p>

			<p>
				One thing the experiment clarified for me was the idea of
				ownership. I already knew that I wanted the visible surface to
				be separate from the voxel structure underneath it, but working
				through this system made the relationship more concrete. Surface
				geometry still has to belong somewhere. If the underlying
				spatial structure is what makes the world editable, then the
				visible pieces need clear rules for which voxel or chunk owns
				them.
			</p>

			<p>
				The bigger idea I want to carry forward is the shift toward
				defining the domain of a surface piece before constructing it.
				My first approach let the intersection between the continuous
				terrain and the voxel lattice decide what geometry was
				available. The columnar approach reversed that: I defined the XZ
				footprint first, then asked how the terrain should be
				represented inside it. That feels like a much more general
				design idea—either work with whatever geometry naturally falls
				out of the underlying representations, or define the region you
				want to cover first and fit the surface into it.
			</p>

			<p>
				The Hermite construction was another useful result. I originally
				arrived there because I wanted some way to make use of the
				height and slope information spread across a tile rather than
				relying on a single sample. It gave me a way to turn that richer
				local information back into the simple planar geometry I was
				looking for, and it also introduced me to a kind of
				interpolation I hadn't worked with before. That feels like a
				technique with uses beyond this terrain experiment.
			</p>

			<p>
				The biggest unresolved question is still the tile shape itself.
				Forcing square tiles onto a continuous terrain surface in 3D was
				always going to be awkward, and this experiment made some of the
				limitations involved very obvious. I still think there may be a
				better way to do this without producing the stretching and
				jagged transitions I ended up with here. I just don't know what
				that construction looks like yet.
			</p>

			<p>
				So although I would approach the final surface construction
				differently, several ideas from this experiment are worth
				keeping and exploring further: explicit ownership of visible
				terrain, defining surface domains up front, reasoning in terms
				of coverage, and using richer local surface information before
				reducing it into simpler geometry.
			</p>
		</section>
	</article>

	<section class="article-project">
		<h2>Related Project</h2>

		<a class="project-card" href="/projects/plane-voxel-lab">
			<h3>Plane Voxel Lab</h3>

			<p>
				An experimental C++ / OpenGL lab for exploring voxel terrain
				representation, surface construction, rendering, and editable
				world systems.
			</p>
		</a>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
